„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙRAnd when they had brought their ships to land, they forsook all, and followed him.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book)§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¼And it came to pass, when he was in a certain city, behold a man full of leprosy: who seeing Jesus fell on his face, and besought him, saying, Lord, if thou wilt, thou canst make me clean.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙyAnd he put forth his hand, and touched him, saying, I will: be thou clean. And immediately the leprosy departed from him.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ And he charged him to tell no man: but go, and shew thyself to the priest, and offer for thy cleansing, according as Moses commanded, for a testimony unto them.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book)§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses’„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙBut so much the more went there a fame abroad of him: and great multitudes came together to hear, and to be healed by him of their infirmities.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ8And he withdrew himself into the wilderness, and prayed.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book)§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses”„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙóAnd it came to pass on a certain day, as he was teaching, that there were Pharisees and doctors of the law sitting by, which were come out of every town of Galilee, and JudÃ¦a, and Jerusalem: and the power of the Lord was present to heal them.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ‡And, behold, men brought in a bed a man which was taken with a palsy: and they sought means to bring him in, and to lay him before him.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÇAnd when they could not find by what way they might bring him in because of the multitude, they went upon the housetop, and let him down through the tiling with his couch into the midst before Jesus.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙOAnd when he saw their faith, he said unto him, Man, thy sins are forgiven thee.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book)§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses”„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ‡And the scribes and the Pharisees began to reason, saying, Who is this which speaketh blasphemies? Who can forgive sins, but God alone?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙdBut when Jesus perceived their thoughts, he answering said unto them, What reason ye in your hearts?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙRWhether is easier, to say, Thy sins be forgiven thee; or to say, Rise up and walk?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ½But that ye may know that the Son of man hath power upon earth to forgive sins, (he said unto the sick of the palsy,) I say unto thee, Arise, and take up thy couch, and go into thine house.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book)§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses’„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙwAnd immediately he rose up before them, and took up that whereon he lay, and departed to his own house, glorifying God.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙxAnd they were all amazed, and they glorified God, and were filled with fear, saying, We have seen strange things to day.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book)§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses’„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙˆAnd after these things he went forth, and saw a publican, named Levi, sitting at the receipt of custom: and he said unto him, Follow me.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ+And he left all, rose up, and followed him.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book)§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse ¦verses”„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙƒAnd Levi made him a great feast in his own house: and there was a great company of publicans and of others that sat down with them.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ{But their scribes and Pharisees murmured against his disciples, saying, Why do ye eat and drink with publicans and sinners?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙeAnd Jesus answering said unto them, They that are whole need not a physician; but they that are sick.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse ¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ<I came not to call the righteous, but sinners to repentance.¦layout ªlast_verse …¤book)§chapter«first_verse!ªlast_verse#¦verses“„¥verse!¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ˜And they said unto him, Why do the disciples of John fast often, and make prayers, and likewise the disciples of the Pharisees; but thine eat and drink?¦layout ªlast_verse!„¥verse"¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙlAnd he said unto them, Can ye make the children of the bridechamber fast, while the bridegroom is with them?¦layout ªlast_verse"„¥verse#¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙrBut the days will come, when the bridegroom shall be taken away from them, and then shall they fast in those days.¦layout ªlast_verse#…¤book)§chapter«first_verse$ªlast_verse'¦verses”„¥verse$¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙĞAnd he spake also a parable unto them; No man putteth a piece of a new garment upon an old; if otherwise, then both the new maketh a rent, and the piece that was taken out of the new agreeth not with the old.¦layout ªlast_verse$„¥verse%¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ…And no man putteth new wine into old bottles; else the new wine will burst the bottles, and be spilled, and the bottles shall perish.¦layout ªlast_verse%„¥verse&¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙBBut new wine must be put into new bottles; and both are preserved.¦layout ªlast_verse&„¥verse'¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ\No man also having drunk old wine straightway desireth new: for he saith, The old is better.¦layout ªlast_verse'